   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "formule",
     "title": "Quadratic",
     "md": "If $\\alpha,\\beta$ are the roots of $ax^2+bx+c=0$: $\\alpha+\\beta=-\\frac ba$, $\\alpha\\beta=\\frac ca$. Then $\\alpha^2+\\beta^2=(\\alpha+\\beta)^2-2\\alpha\\beta$ and $\\frac1\\alpha+\\frac1\\beta=\\frac{\\alpha+\\beta}{\\alpha\\beta}$."
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "formule",
     "title": "Cubic",
     "md": "If $\\alpha,\\beta,\\gamma$ are the roots of $ax^3+bx^2+cx+d=0$: $\\alpha+\\beta+\\gamma=-\\frac ba$, $\\alpha\\beta+\\beta\\gamma+\\gamma\\alpha=\\frac ca$, $\\alpha\\beta\\gamma=-\\frac da$. Then $\\alpha^2+\\beta^2+\\gamma^2=(\\alpha+\\beta+\\gamma)^2-2(\\alpha\\beta+\\beta\\gamma+\\gamma\\alpha)$."
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "methode",
     "title": "New equations",
     "md": "To find the equation whose roots are, say, $\\frac1\\alpha,\\frac1\\beta$: find the sum and product of the NEW roots using the old sum and product, then write $x^2-(\\text{sum})x+(\\text{product})=0$. Or substitute $x=\\frac1y$ (or $y=2x$, etc.) into the original equation."
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "pieges",
     "title": "Common mistakes",
     "md": "Signs: the sum of the roots is $-\\frac ba$, the product of the roots of a cubic is $-\\frac da$. Remember to multiply by the leading coefficient $a$ at the end if integer coefficients are asked."
    },
    {
     "type": "example",
     "title": "Example 1",
     "statement": "The roots of $2x^2-5x+1=0$ are $\\alpha,\\beta$. Find $\\alpha^2+\\beta^2$ and the equation with roots $\\frac1\\alpha,\\frac1\\beta$.",
     "steps": [
      "$\\alpha+\\beta=\\frac{5}{2}$, $\\alpha\\beta=\\frac{1}{2}$. $\\alpha^2+\\beta^2=\\frac{25}{4}-1=\\frac{21}{4}$.",
      "New sum $=\\frac{5/2}{1/2}=5$; new product $=\\frac{1}{1/2}=2$. Equation $x^2-5x+2=0$."
     ],
     "answer": "$\\frac{21}{4}$; $x^2-5x+2=0$"
    },
    {
